\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces  Comparison of representative approaches related to vergence (V) and accommodation (A) sensing. Colours rate each entry \emph  {relative to the other options in its column} with respect to one goal, a compact wearable sensor that estimates V and A jointly; green is better suited, yellow intermediate, red least suited. Camera: imaging hardware per eye, ordered by the minimum needed for binocular V and A -- one camera per eye (the lower bound, since V requires both eyes and A one) $>$ two or more sensors per eye $>$ bench-top optics with head stabilisation. This ordering reflects footprint, not accuracy: head-fixed bench systems and multi-view rigs achieve lower errors by removing or measuring eye--camera motion, whereas a single camera must rely on illumination layout and eye-model priors (see Accuracy). Illum.: integrability and designability of the source geometry -- freely positionable IR/NIR LEDs, whose number and placement are chosen for the sensing principle, $>$ LEDs fixed inside a commercial tracker, placed by the vendor for corneal glints and not adjustable, $>$ bench-top collimated or off-axis sources tied to a working distance. As for Camera, this reflects integrability, not image quality: collimated bench sources yield the sharpest lens reflections. Method: whether the signal physically depends on lens state and can be captured by a compact camera -- Purkinje reflections (lens-dependent, camera and LED only) $>$ photorefraction (lens-dependent, but requires an eccentric source at a bench working distance) $>$ pupil/glint gaze tracking (no A information). Data: validated on real eyes $>$ synthetic only. Learning: handling of inter-subject variability -- learned models $>$ physical eye models requiring per-user calibration $>$ fixed formulas such as binocular triangulation. V/A Target: quantity directly estimated (both $>$ one $>$ neither). Near-eye: demonstrated in a physical near-eye configuration. Accuracy: error reported for the quantity in parentheses -- gaze: high $<0.5^\circ $, mid $0.5$--$3^\circ $, low $>3^\circ $; A and V (depth in dioptres): high $<0.25$\nonbreakingspace D, mid $0.25$--$0.5$\nonbreakingspace D, low $>0.5$\nonbreakingspace D; protocols differ across works, so values are indicative only. }}{2}{table.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{tab:literature_review}{{1}{2}{Comparison of representative approaches related to vergence (V) and accommodation (A) sensing. Colours rate each entry \emph {relative to the other options in its column} with respect to one goal, a compact wearable sensor that estimates V and A jointly; green is better suited, yellow intermediate, red least suited. Camera: imaging hardware per eye, ordered by the minimum needed for binocular V and A -- one camera per eye (the lower bound, since V requires both eyes and A one) $>$ two or more sensors per eye $>$ bench-top optics with head stabilisation. This ordering reflects footprint, not accuracy: head-fixed bench systems and multi-view rigs achieve lower errors by removing or measuring eye--camera motion, whereas a single camera must rely on illumination layout and eye-model priors (see Accuracy). Illum.: integrability and designability of the source geometry -- freely positionable IR/NIR LEDs, whose number and placement are chosen for the sensing principle, $>$ LEDs fixed inside a commercial tracker, placed by the vendor for corneal glints and not adjustable, $>$ bench-top collimated or off-axis sources tied to a working distance. As for Camera, this reflects integrability, not image quality: collimated bench sources yield the sharpest lens reflections. Method: whether the signal physically depends on lens state and can be captured by a compact camera -- Purkinje reflections (lens-dependent, camera and LED only) $>$ photorefraction (lens-dependent, but requires an eccentric source at a bench working distance) $>$ pupil/glint gaze tracking (no A information). Data: validated on real eyes $>$ synthetic only. Learning: handling of inter-subject variability -- learned models $>$ physical eye models requiring per-user calibration $>$ fixed formulas such as binocular triangulation. V/A Target: quantity directly estimated (both $>$ one $>$ neither). Near-eye: demonstrated in a physical near-eye configuration. Accuracy: error reported for the quantity in parentheses -- gaze: high $<0.5^\circ $, mid $0.5$--$3^\circ $, low $>3^\circ $; A and V (depth in dioptres): high $<0.25$~D, mid $0.25$--$0.5$~D, low $>0.5$~D; protocols differ across works, so values are indicative only}{table.caption.5}{}}
