\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces  Eye representations and rendering approaches relevant to modelling Purkinje reflections. Colours rate each entry relative to the other options in its column with respect to one goal, an eye model that can be fitted to a real eye and renders Purkinje reflections for training or fitting a V/A estimator; green is better suited, yellow intermediate, red least suited. Representation: explicit optical surfaces combined with image-fitted neural appearance $>$ explicit surfaces from population parameters that cannot be fitted to an individual (optical eye models, parametric meshes) $>$ purely neural fields without surfaces for reflection or refraction. Reflections: ray-traced or analytic specular reflections $>$ reflection-aware shading (reflected-direction parametrisation, environment maps) $>$ baked view-dependent appearance or rasterised highlights. Input: images needed to personalise the model to a real eye -- few images $>$ dense multi-view or light-stage capture $>$ synthetic only, not personalisable. Fidelity: reported agreement with real images -- quantitative (PSNR/SSIM) $>$ photorealistic without quantitative comparison $>$ geometric or appearance approximation. Speed: real-time ($\geq $30\nonbreakingspace fps) $>$ interactive (1--30\nonbreakingspace fps) $>$ offline. Gaze and Lens: whether eye rotation and lens (accommodative) state are explicit controllable variables. }}{3}{table.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:eye_rendering}{{2}{3}{Eye representations and rendering approaches relevant to modelling Purkinje reflections. Colours rate each entry relative to the other options in its column with respect to one goal, an eye model that can be fitted to a real eye and renders Purkinje reflections for training or fitting a V/A estimator; green is better suited, yellow intermediate, red least suited. Representation: explicit optical surfaces combined with image-fitted neural appearance $>$ explicit surfaces from population parameters that cannot be fitted to an individual (optical eye models, parametric meshes) $>$ purely neural fields without surfaces for reflection or refraction. Reflections: ray-traced or analytic specular reflections $>$ reflection-aware shading (reflected-direction parametrisation, environment maps) $>$ baked view-dependent appearance or rasterised highlights. Input: images needed to personalise the model to a real eye -- few images $>$ dense multi-view or light-stage capture $>$ synthetic only, not personalisable. Fidelity: reported agreement with real images -- quantitative (PSNR/SSIM) $>$ photorealistic without quantitative comparison $>$ geometric or appearance approximation. Speed: real-time ($\geq $30~fps) $>$ interactive (1--30~fps) $>$ offline. Gaze and Lens: whether eye rotation and lens (accommodative) state are explicit controllable variables}{table.caption.6}{}}
